<h2 id="bring-your-schedule-into-helium">Bring Your Schedule into Helium</h2>

<p>With your calendar's <code>.ics</code> file or URL, there are two ways to bring it into Helium:</p>

<ul>
  <li>
    <a href="/support/import-export-and-backup/importing-and-exporting-your-data/#importing-a-calendar-ics-file"><strong>Import the file</strong></a>.
    Its entries become assignments in a class, which you can check off and grade, or events.
  </li>
  <li>
    <a href="/support/sync-and-integration/how-to-sync-google-apple-or-other-calendars-into-helium-using-external-calendars/"><strong>Subscribe to it</strong></a>
    as an External Calendar. It stays up to date as long as the source does, but its entries are read-only in Helium.
  </li>
</ul>

<p>
  No calendar? <a href="/support/getting-started/where-to-start-with-helium/#connect-your-ai-assistant"><strong>Connect your AI assistant</strong></a>
  to Helium and give it your syllabi, or your list of classes, meeting times, and assignments. It sets up your term in
  Helium for you. Prefer to do it yourself? The <a href="/support/getting-started/where-to-start-with-helium/#set-up-your-first-term"><strong>manual walkthrough</strong></a>
  gets you to a usable term step by step.
</p>

<blockquote>
  <p><strong>Note:</strong> Helium is free and open source, including cross-device sync. To see what Helium does, <a href="https://www.heliumedu.com/#features">check out our homepage</a>.</p>
</blockquote>
